Turkey's biggest city Istanbul saw  thousands of demonstrators demand the  release of women imprisoned in Syria the  organizers say that more than 13 and a  half thousand women have been jailed  there since the Civil War began almost  eight years ago and that more than 7,000  of them have been subject to torture  rape or other inhumane treatment no DW  correspondent Julia Hahn is in Istanbul  close to the famous attacks in square  where the main women's March is taking  place welcome  Yulia what's been happening well right  I'm in the city centre and we had to  move a little further away from the  crowd we've witnessed some quite heated  scenes just recently the police has been  trying to disperse the crowd right in  the back I'm not sure whether you can  see that there is a huge police a  blockade policemen are and women are  heavily armed  today so you're just seeing a small part  of the crowd of the actual crowd that we  saw today people women were actually  trying to walk down March Town Istiklal  Avenue a really meaningful place but as  I mentioned they were blocked by police  so they were basically gathering for one  or two hours hundreds of people  definitely maybe thousands it's really  hard to guess from looking at the scenes  here last year women were still allowed  to march down Istiqlal this year as I  said the police intervened and called  people now to disperse and that's  basically what we see right now people  are trying to leave the city centre  through the three side streets  okay so police who have dispersed it but  they allowed this mass gathering to  start in the first place and mass  protests have been banned there in  Turkey since that the coup in 2016 why  did they allow today's to go ahead well  first of all it's of course they're  internationals women stay  it's a meaningful protest but as you  rightly said especially since the failed  school here in Turkey in summer 2016 we  have witnessed a massive crackdown on  media organizations civil society  organizations on private persons as well  we have been criticizing the Turkish  government or president out on himself  so all kinds of demonstrations be it  workers unions demonstrations the Gay  Pride they have all been banned so that  shows how difficult it has become for  many people to voice their concerns that  their anger how little space there is  left and I think today tonight is just  another example and that's why actually  Women's Day is so important for many  people here not just for women but for  many others as well  Yulia Hahn in Istanbul thank you
 

